  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.
      Double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge. If needed, use the “^” symbol to enter
      an exponent.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Whenever the input of a function \(f\) increases by 1, the output increases by 5. Which of these equations could
    define \(f\)? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\(f(x)=3x+5\)</item>
  <item>\(f(x)=5x+3\)</item>
  <item>\( f(x)=5^x\)</item>
  <item>\( f(x)=x^5\) </item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(f(x)=5x+3\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>The function \(f\) is defined by \(f(x)=2^x\). Which of the following statements is true about the values of
    \(f\)? Select all that apply.  </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> When the input \(x\) increases by 1, the value of \(f\) increases by 2.</item>
  <item>When the input \(x\) increases by 1, the value of \(f\) increases by a factor of 2.</item>
  <item>When the input \(x\) increases by 3, the value of \(f\) increases by 8.</item>
  <item> When the input \(x\) increases by 3, the value of \(f\) increases by a factor of 8.</item>
  <item> When the input \(x\)increases by 4, the value of \(f\) increases by a factor of 4.</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> When the input \(x\) increases by 1, the value of \(f\) increases by a factor of 2 and d:
  When the input \(x\)increases by 3, the value of \(f\) increases by a factor of 8. </para>
<para>The two lines on the coordinate plane are graphs of functions \(f\) and \(g\). Use for questions 3 and 4.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of 2 lines."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/12724ad9231ed2d7dd1720cc90447eb9c5fa3e21"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the graph to explain why the value of \(f\) increases by 2 each time the input \(x\) increases by 1. 
  </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> The \(y\)-axis counts by 2’s so f must increase 2 each time the input increases 1. </item>
  <item> There are two graphs so \(f\) increases by 2 each time the input \(x\) increases by 1. </item>
  <item> (0,0) and (1,2) are two points on the graph of \(f\). Between the two points, \(y\) increases by 2 and \(x\)
    increases by 1. </item>
  <item> \(f\)does not increase by 2 each time \(x\) increases by 1. </item>
</list>

<para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> (0,0) and (1,2) are two points on the graph of \(f\). Between the two points, \(y\)
  increases by 2 and \(x\) increases by 1. </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the graph to explain why the value of \(g\) increases by 2 each time the input \(x\) increases by 1. Select
    all that apply. </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> \(f\) and \(g\) never intersect and stay an equal distance apart. \(f\)<emphasis> </emphasis>increases by 2
    every time \(x\) increases by 1, so \(g\)  must do so as well. </item>
  <item> You can’t use the graph because the points are not labeled </item>
  <item> There are two graphs so \(g\) increases by 2 each time the input \(x\) increases by 1. </item>
  <item> (0,5) and (1,7) are points on the graph of \(g\)<emphasis>.</emphasis>  Between the two points, \(y\) increases by 2
    and \(x\) increases by 1. </item>
  <item> (0,0) and (1,2) are two points on the graph of \(g\)<emphasis>. </emphasis> Between the two points,
    \(y\) increases by 2 and \(x\) increases by 1 </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \(f\) and \(g\) never intersect and stay an equal distance apart. \(f\) increases by 2
  every time \(x\) increases by 1, so \(g\) has to also. And d. (0,5) and (1,7) are points on the graph of \(g\). 
  Between the two points, \(y\) increases by 2 and \(x\) increases by 1.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>The function \(h\) is given by \(h(x)=5^x\). Find the quotient \(\frac{h(x+2)}{h(x)}\). </item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>25 </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>The function h is given by \(h(x)=5^x\). Find the quotient \(\frac{h(x+3)}{h(x)}\). What does this tell you about
    how the value of \(h\) changes when the input is increased by 3?</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 125 </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>For each of the functions \(f, g, h, p,\) and \(q\), the domain is \(0≤x≤100\). For which functions is the
    average rate of change a good measure of how the function changes for this domain? Select all that apply. </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item>\( f(x)=x+2\)</item>
  <item>\(g(x)=2^x\)</item>
  <item>\(h(x)=111x−23\)</item>
  <item>\(p(x)=50,000 \cdot 3^x\)</item>
  <item>\(q(x)=87.5\)</item>
</list>
<para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(f(x)=x+2\), \(h(x)=111x−23\), \(q(x)=87.5\) </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item>What would be the rate of change of the following table?</item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        \(x\)
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        \(f(x)\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        -4
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        -2
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        5
      </entry>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<para> <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 2 </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item>Given the following table, what would be \(\frac {f(x+2)}{f(x+1)}\)?</item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        \(x\)
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
        \(f(x)\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        8
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry>
        16
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        5
      </entry>
      <entry>
        32
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 2 </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of the following statements compare rates of \(f(x) = 3x +2\) and \(g(x)=3^x\)? </item>
</list>
<list>
  <item> In \(f\) and \(g\) you multiply by 3 to get to the next term </item>
  <item> In \(f\) and \(g\) you add 3 to get to the next term </item>
  <item> In \(f\) you add 3 to get to the next term; in \(g\) you multiply by 3 to get to the next term. </item>
  <item> In \(g\) you add 3 to get to the next term; in \(f\) you multiply by 3 to get to the next term. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> In \(f\) you add 3 to get to the next term in \(g\) you multiply by 3 to get to the next
  term. </para></section>
